/*  A program written across two files, holding a type that belongs to the other one.

    `charge` is declared inside the `bookkeeping` module in bookkeeping.cufe. Pulling the module
    brings the type into scope BY ITS SHORT NAME, for the length of the block — the same way
    `matrix` arrives with `Pull a book on collections.` Outside the block the name is gone.

    ★ Two pulls, nested, because they are different kinds of thing. `math` is a book: you consult
    it, and it says `and book`. `bookkeeping` is a module you have one of. One statement cannot
    pull both — `Pull books on …` is the book spelling and applies to every name in it — so they
    nest, outermost first.

    ★ The nesting is not ceremony here: `bookkeeping`'s `rounded-total` names `math` without
    pulling it, and this block is what supplies it. Take the outer pull away and the program is
    refused AT THE PULL, naming what is missing, rather than dying part way through.  */

Pull a book on math.
    Pull a bookkeeping.
        Define spending as a series of charge with (
            a new charge { the note "coffee", the amount 3.5 },
            a new charge { the note "notebook", the amount 24.99 },
            a new charge { the note "train fare", the amount 8.256 }).

        For each item in spending, repeat:
            State cast item's line.
        Done.

        State "".
        State "exact:   {cast bookkeeping's total on (spending)}".
        State "rounded: {cast bookkeeping's rounded-total on (spending)}".
    Done.
Done.
